  "final_message": "I haven't changed anything yet, because this feature conflicts with ARCH-5.\n\nARCH-5 says this service may only call what the architecture catalog declares, and today that is only payments-api. `stock.inventory.internal` would be a new runtime dependency. It has to be declared in the catalog and approved by Architecture first. I can't find the catalog in this repo, and nothing in your request says approval exists. If I wired the call in as the default, the service would start calling an undeclared system once it's deployed.\n\nHow do you want to proceed?\n\n1. **Approval exists or is on its way.** Give me the ticket or catalog entry. I'll implement the whole feature:\n   - a `StockGateway` port in `src/core/ports.ts`\n   - an `HttpStockGateway` adapter in `src/adapters/`, matching `HttpPaymentGateway` (timeout, `fetchFunction` injection)\n   - a stock check in `OrderService.placeOrder`, run before payment authorization so a rejected order never triggers a charge\n   - an `InsufficientStockError` that the error mapper turns into 409\n   - an optional `stockGateway` dep in `createApp`\n   - tests, including a fake gateway in `test/helpers.ts`\n\n2. **Build it now, ship it later.** I implement everything above, but the default wiring doesn't call the real API until the approval lands. For example, it could be switched on by configuration, or the default could be a gateway that always reports stock as available. That gives you a reviewable change without an undeclared call in production.\n\nWhichever you pick, I need your call on one behavior: what should happen when the Stock API is down or times out? I could fail the order with a 502, as the payment gateway does, or let the order through. I'd suggest 502, so we never oversell silently.\n\nThe 409 itself is additive and doesn't touch the `OrderPlaced`/`OrderCancelled` events or `GET /orders/{id}`, so ARCH-4 and ARCH-8 aren't affected.",
